## Analysis

### Q1: Which circuits sit at the highest altitude?

**Approach:** Sort by altitude descending and keep the top 10. `NULLS LAST` protects against missing values pushing to the top of a descending sort. There are none in this file, but it is a good habit.

```sql
SELECT name, altitude
FROM circuits
ORDER BY altitude DESC NULLS LAST
LIMIT 10;
```

**Result:**

| Rank | Circuit | Altitude (m) |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Autódromo Hermanos Rodríguez | 2,227 |
| 2 | Kyalami | 1,460 |
| 3 | Autódromo Internacional Nelson Piquet | 1,126 |
| 4 | Charade Circuit | 790 |
| 5 | Autódromo José Carlos Pace | 785 |
| 6 | Red Bull Ring | 678 |
| 7 | Zeltweg | 676 |
| 8 | Las Vegas Strip Street Circuit | 642 |
| 9 | Las Vegas Street Circuit | 639 |
| 10 | Jarama | 609 |

**Takeaway:** The top circuit is more than 700 m higher than the second. There are no ties at the 10th spot (the next circuit is Fuji Speedway at 583 m), so `LIMIT 10` does not cut anyone off unfairly. If ties were possible, `FETCH FIRST 10 ROWS WITH TIES` would keep them.

